### What is a prefix sum?

A prefix sum array stores at each index the cumulative total of all elements from the beginning up to that index. Once built in O(n) time, it allows any range sum query to be answered in O(1) time using a single subtraction.

At the National Bank of India, Rajesh's daily deposits for a closed month are stored in an array. The statement tool needs to compute the total deposits between any two dates. Instead of re-adding each time, we build a prefix sum once and answer every query with one subtraction.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class PrefixSumDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] dailyDeposits = {100, 200, 50, 300, 150};
        int[] prefix = buildPrefix(dailyDeposits);
        System.out.println("Deposits: " + Arrays.toString(dailyDeposits));
        System.out.println("Prefix:   " + Arrays.toString(prefix));
        System.out.println("Sum [1..3]: " + rangeSum(prefix, 1, 3));  // 200+50+300 = 550
    }

    static int[] buildPrefix(int[] arr) {
        int[] prefix = new int[arr.length + 1];
        prefix[0] = 0;  // leading zero
        for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
            prefix[i + 1] = prefix[i] + arr[i];
        }
        return prefix;
    }

    static int rangeSum(int[] prefix, int left, int right) {
        return prefix[right + 1] - prefix[left];
    }
}
```

```text
Deposits: [100, 200, 50, 300, 150]
Prefix:   [0, 100, 300, 350, 650, 800]
Sum [1..3]: 550
```

### Why does the leading zero matter?

The prefix array has length n+1 with `prefix[0] = 0`. This means `prefix[i]` is the sum of the first i elements, with prefix[0] being the sum of zero elements. The range formula `prefix[right+1] - prefix[left]` works cleanly without special-casing the start of the array.

```java
// Without leading zero, you need a special case for left=0:
int sum = (left == 0) ? prefix[right] : prefix[right] - prefix[left - 1];

// With leading zero, no special case needed:
int sum = prefix[right + 1] - prefix[left];
```

### How does the range query work mathematically?

The prefix value at position right+1 is the sum of everything from index 0 to right. The prefix value at position left is the sum of everything from index 0 to left-1. Subtracting cancels the part we do not want and leaves exactly the elements from left to right.

```text
prefix[6] - prefix[2] = sum of all 6 elements - sum of first 2 elements
                       = (arr[0]+arr[1]+arr[2]+arr[3]+arr[4]+arr[5])
                       - (arr[0]+arr[1])
                       = arr[2]+arr[3]+arr[4]+arr[5]
```

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class RangeQueryExplained {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60};
        int[] prefix = {0, 10, 30, 60, 100, 150, 210};

        // Sum of arr[2..5] = prefix[6] - prefix[2] = 210 - 30 = 180
        System.out.println("Sum [2..5]: " + (prefix[6] - prefix[2]));
        // Verify: 30+40+50+60 = 180
        System.out.println("Verify: " + (30 + 40 + 50 + 60));
    }
}
```

```text
Sum [2..5]: 180
Verify: 180
```

### What is the performance gain?

Without prefix sums, each range query scans the array from left to right, costing O(n). For q queries, the total cost is O(n*q). With prefix sums, the O(n) build is done once, and each query is O(1), so the total cost is O(n+q).

| Approach | Build | Per Query | q Queries |
|----------|-------|-----------|-----------|
| Naive re-sum | O(1) | O(n) | O(n*q) |
| Prefix sum | O(n) | O(1) | O(n+q) |

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class PerformanceComparison {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 1_000_000;
        int q = 100_000;
        int[] arr = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) arr[i] = i + 1;

        // Prefix sum approach
        long start = System.nanoTime();
        long[] prefix = new long[n + 1];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) prefix[i + 1] = prefix[i] + arr[i];
        long total = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < q; i++) {
            total += prefix[n] - prefix[i % n];
        }
        long elapsed = System.nanoTime() - start;
        System.out.println("Prefix sum: " + (elapsed / 1_000_000) + " ms");
    }
}
```

### How do you use BigDecimal for money?

For banking calculations, the prefix array uses `BigDecimal` with `add` and `subtract` instead of primitive `+` and `-`. This keeps totals exact and avoids floating-point errors on money.

```java
package com.bank.statement;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Arrays;

public class StatementRangeSum {

    static BigDecimal[] buildPrefix(BigDecimal[] dailyAmounts) {
        BigDecimal[] prefix = new BigDecimal[dailyAmounts.length + 1];
        prefix[0] = BigDecimal.ZERO;
        for (int i = 0; i < dailyAmounts.length; i++) {
            prefix[i + 1] = prefix[i].add(dailyAmounts[i]);
        }
        return prefix;
    }

    static BigDecimal rangeSum(BigDecimal[] prefix, int left, int right) {
        return prefix[right + 1].subtract(prefix[left]);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BigDecimal[] deposits = {
            new BigDecimal("15000.50"),
            new BigDecimal("25000.75"),
            new BigDecimal("10000.00"),
            new BigDecimal("50000.25"),
            new BigDecimal("30000.00")
        };
        BigDecimal[] prefix = buildPrefix(deposits);
        System.out.println("Deposits: " + Arrays.toString(deposits));
        System.out.println("Sum [1..3]: " + rangeSum(prefix, 1, 3));
    }
}
```

```text
Deposits: [15000.50, 25000.75, 10000.00, 50000.25, 30000.00]
Sum [1..3]: 85001.00
```

### What about 2D prefix sums?

Prefix sums extend to two dimensions for submatrix sum queries. The 2D prefix stores cumulative sums over rows and columns, and a submatrix sum uses inclusion-exclusion with four prefix values.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class PrefixSum2D {
    static int[][] buildPrefix2D(int[][] matrix) {
        int rows = matrix.length, cols = matrix[0].length;
        int[][] p = new int[rows + 1][cols + 1];
        for (int r = 1; r <= rows; r++) {
            for (int c = 1; c <= cols; c++) {
                p[r][c] = matrix[r-1][c-1]
                    + p[r-1][c] + p[r][c-1] - p[r-1][c-1];
            }
        }
        return p;
    }

    static int submatrixSum(int[][] p, int r1, int c1, int r2, int c2) {
        return p[r2+1][c2+1] - p[r1][c2+1] - p[r2+1][c1] + p[r1][c1];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[][] matrix = {{1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6}, {7, 8, 9}};
        int[][] p = buildPrefix2D(matrix);
        System.out.println("Submatrix [1,1]->[2,2]: " + submatrixSum(p, 1, 1, 2, 2));
        // 5+6+8+9 = 28
    }
}
```

```text
Submatrix [1,1]->[2,2]: 28
```

### When should you NOT use prefix sums?

Prefix sums assume the underlying array does not change between queries. If the array is frequently updated, rebuilding the prefix array costs O(n) per update, which defeats the purpose. For dynamic data, use a Fenwick tree (Binary Indexed Tree) for O(log n) updates and queries, or a segment tree for more complex operations.

```java
// Static data: prefix sum is perfect
// Closed-month statement, historical prices, fixed scores

// Dynamic data: use Fenwick tree instead
// Real-time balance updates, live trading data, streaming counts
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is an off-by-one error in the range formula. Without the leading zero, the formula changes and developers mix up inclusive vs exclusive bounds.

```java
// WRONG: forgetting leading zero
int[] prefix = new int[n];  // no leading zero
prefix[0] = arr[0];
for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) prefix[i] = prefix[i-1] + arr[i];
// Range query: sum [left..right]
int sum = prefix[right] - (left > 0 ? prefix[left - 1] : 0);  // special case needed!

// CORRECT: with leading zero
int[] prefix = new int[n + 1];
prefix[0] = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) prefix[i + 1] = prefix[i] + arr[i];
int sum = prefix[right + 1] - prefix[left];  // clean, no special case
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Prefix XOR, prefix count, prefix product.** The concept generalizes to any cumulative aggregate that supports an inverse operation. Prefix XOR answers range XOR queries; prefix count answers range frequency queries.
- **Immutability is the key assumption.** Once the data changes, the prefix array is stale. This is why prefix sums pair well with closed-period data in banking (closed-month statements, historical price series).
- **Overflow risk.** For large arrays with large values, use `long` instead of `int` for the prefix array to avoid overflow.
